    <!-- Task 15: Trek Safety Tips -->
    <string name="safety_tips_title">Trek safety tips</string>
    <string name="safety_tips_subtitle">Essential guidelines for a safe mountain adventure</string>
    <string name="safety_tips_emergency_section">Emergency Assistance (India)</string>
    <string name="safety_tips_emergency_number">112</string>
    <string name="safety_tips_emergency_desc">National Emergency Number in India. Connects to police, fire, and medical rescue services.</string>
    <string name="safety_tips_emergency_button">Call 112 (Emergency)</string>
    <string name="safety_tips_emergency_action_label">open dialer for 112</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_1_title">1. Monitor Mountain Weather</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_1_desc">Mountain conditions change in minutes. Check updated forecasts before starting and be prepared to turn back if severe weather approaches.</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_2_title">2. Stay Hydrated &amp; Purify Water</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_2_desc">Drink 3 to 4 liters of water each day. Always filter or use purification tablets on natural mountain streams to prevent illness.</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_3_title">3. Acclimatise Gradually</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_3_desc">Ascend slowly above 2,500 meters (climb high, sleep low). Never push through acute mountain sickness (AMS) symptoms such as dizziness or headaches.</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_4_title">4. Share Your Trek Plan</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_4_desc">Always share your exact route, planned campsites, return schedule, and your guide\'s contact info with family or trusted friends.</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_5_title">5. Dress in Technical Layers</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_5_desc">Wear a moisture-wicking base layer, an insulating fleece mid-layer, and a wind/waterproof outer shell. Avoid cotton fabrics.</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_6_title">6. Stay on Marked Trails</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_6_desc">Never take unofficial shortcuts or wander off designated trails. Mountain terrain is steep and trails can quickly become disorienting in mist.</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_7_title">7. Carry Essential Safety Gear</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_7_desc">Keep a reliable headlamp with extra batteries, whistle, emergency thermal blanket, basic first-aid kit, and personal medications in your pack.</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_8_title">8. Respect Your Certified Guide</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_8_desc">Your guide is trained in mountain safety and local geography. Always heed their advice on pacing, rest breaks, and weather turnarounds.</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_9_title">9. Set a Strict Turnaround Time</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_9_desc">Agree on a firm turnaround time before embarking on summit pushes, and stick to it regardless of how close you are to the top.</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_10_title">10. Leave No Trace</string>
    <string name="safety_tip_10_desc">Carry all waste back down, respect mountain wildlife and alpine flora, and keep trail water sources unpolluted for other trekkers.</string>

